      "Bash(perl -0777 -pi -e 's/- \\\\[ \\\\] \\\\*\\\\*🔴 Node removal is broken and cannot stick\\\\. Caused a production\\\\n      outage\\\\.\\\\*\\\\*/- [x] **Node removal now sticks, and no longer double-runs workloads.**\\\\n      \\(Caused a production outage.\\)/' ROADMAP.md)",
      "Bash(perl -0777 -pi -e 's/      The fix has three parts:\\\\n      1\\\\. \\\\*\\\\*Peer tombstones\\\\*\\\\*/      Fixed with tombstones plus a cooperative leave:\\\\n      1. **Peer tombstones**/' ROADMAP.md)",
      "Bash(perl -0777 -pi -e 's/      Also: do not tear down tunnels\\\\/routes until the leave is acknowledged or\\\\n      the node is confirmed unreachable\\\\. Today the teardown happens first,\\\\n      unconditionally\\\\./      Teardown now happens after the leave is requested, and the response\\\\n      reports `leave_acknowledged` so an operator knows whether the node\\\\n      actually heard. Key revocation is NOT done - a removed node keeps its\\\\n      APIKey, and the tombstone is what keeps it out. Worth closing later.\\\\n\\\\n      Not covered: a node that is off when removed and boots weeks later\\\\n      inside the 30-day window will be refused by gossip and needs a fresh\\\\n      join token. That is the intended behaviour, but it will surprise\\\\n      someone./' ROADMAP.md)",
      "Bash(perl -0777 -pi -e 's/\\(\\\\ttunnelMode        string          \\\\/\\\\/ \"ipip\", \"gre\", or \"\" \\\\\\(none available\\\\\\)\\\\n\\)/$1\\\\t\\\\/\\\\/ tunnelStack selects the userspace tunnel receive implementation:\\\\n\\\\t\\\\/\\\\/ \"netstack\" \\(gVisor, real TCP\\) or \"legacy\" \\(the hand-rolled proxy\\).\\\\n\\\\ttunnelStack string\\\\n/' agent/types.go)",
